<body>
<main>
<article>
<header class=\"prose\">
<div class=\"eyebrow\">Distributed systems / field notes 01</div>
<h1>Retries Aren't Idempotent By Accident</h1>
<p class=\"dek\">A retry repeats delivery; an idempotency contract preserves the identity and outcome of the work.</p>
<div class=\"stats\" aria-label=\"The example in numbers\"><span class=\"pill accent\">1 operation key</span><span class=\"pill warn\">2 delivery attempts</span><span class=\"pill ok\">1 committed order</span></div>
</header>
<div class=\"prose intro\">
<p>A client submits an order. The server commits it, but the response disappears behind a broken connection. The client sees a timeout: evidence that it lacks an answer, not evidence that the order failed. Retrying an ordinary POST can create another order. Exponential backoff changes when that happens; it does nothing to change what happens.</p>
<p>Idempotency makes repeated requests converge on one intended effect. For an operation that creates something, that property needs a protocol: the client names its intent, and the server remembers what that intent produced. “Retry and hope” supplies neither identity nor memory.</p>
</div>
<section aria-labelledby=\"identity\">
<div class=\"prose\"><div class=\"tag\">01 / Identity</div><h2 id=\"identity\">Name the intent before sending</h2>
<p>Generate a random operation key before the first attempt and persist it with the pending client operation. Every retry, including one after a client restart, sends that same key. A second intentional purchase gets a new key, even when its basket is identical. A hash of the payload alone cannot distinguish those two purchases; AWS explains this distinction in its <a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/making-retries-safe-with-idempotent-APIs/\">discussion of caller-provided request identifiers</a>.</p>
<p>Scope the server record to the authenticated tenant, operation, and key. Store a fingerprint of validated, normalized parameters alongside it. Reusing the key with a different quantity should produce a conflict, not silently return an unrelated order. Authentication still runs on every attempt; possession of a key never grants access to somebody else’s result.</p>
<p class=\"thesis-lead\">The key identifies one business intention, not one network attempt.</p></div>
<figure class=\"scene\">
<figcaption class=\"scene-label\"><span>Fig. 01 — An answer lost, an order retained</span><span>Same key / same result</span></figcaption>
<div class=\"scene-body\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 800 320\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The client sends POST with key k7, the server commits order 42 but its response is lost; the client retries with k7 and receives the stored result without another order.\">
<defs><marker id=\"arrow\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0L10 5L0 10Z\" fill=\"var(--accent)\"/></marker></defs>
<rect x=\"28\" y=\"10\" width=\"150\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"var(--accent-soft)\"/><text x=\"103\" y=\"35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">CLIENT</text>
<rect x=\"608\" y=\"10\" width=\"160\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"var(--code-bg)\"/><text x=\"688\" y=\"35\" text-anchor=\"middle\">SERVER + DB</text>
<path class=\"life\" d=\"M103 58V312M688 58V312\"/>
<path class=\"wire\" d=\"M103 89H688\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\"/><text x=\"220\" y=\"77\">POST /orders · key=k7</text>
<rect class=\"good\" x=\"525\" y=\"104\" width=\"245\" height=\"36\" rx=\"8\"/><text x=\"647\" y=\"127\" text-anchor=\"middle\">COMMIT order 42 + result</text>
<path class=\"wire\" d=\"M688 167H290\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 6\"/><text x=\"346\" y=\"156\">201 · order 42</text><text class=\"warning\" x=\"116\" y=\"172\">× response lost</text>
<path class=\"wire\" d=\"M103 222H688\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\"/><text x=\"220\" y=\"210\">RETRY /orders · key=k7</text>
<path class=\"wire\" d=\"M688 279H103\" marker-end=\"url(#arrow)\"/><text x=\"220\" y=\"267\">201 · stored order 42</text>
</svg></div>
<div class=\"scene-foot\">The second delivery reads the committed result. It does not create order 43.</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section aria-labelledby=\"atomic\">
<div class=\"prose\"><div class=\"tag\">02 / Atomicity</div><h2 id=\"atomic\">Make the record and effect indivisible</h2>
<p>A cache lookup followed by processing leaves a race: two workers can both observe an absent key. A durable unique constraint must arbitrate ownership. In this database-only example, the claim, order insert, and saved response share one transaction. PostgreSQL’s <a href=\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-insert.html\">ON CONFLICT mechanism</a> supplies the competing-insert behavior.</p></div>
<figure class=\"scene\">
<figcaption class=\"scene-label\"><span>Listing 01 — POST /orders</span><span>Python-style pseudocode</span></figcaption>
<div class=\"scene-body\"><pre><code># Authenticate and validate first. Reject a missing key.
# UNIQUE (tenant_id, operation, key) on requests.
# claim(): INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING RETURNING *
# PostgreSQL READ COMMITTED; conflicting inserts wait.
scope = (auth.tenant_id, \"create_order\", request.key)
fingerprint = sha256(canonical_json(validated_body))

with db.transaction(isolation=\"READ COMMITTED\") as tx:
    owned = tx.claim(scope, fingerprint)
    if not owned:
        previous = tx.get_request(scope)  # new statement snapshot
        if previous.fingerprint "'!= fingerprint:
            raise Conflict("Key reused with different parameters")
        response = previous.response
    else:
        order = tx.insert_order(validated_body)
        response = (201, {"id": order.id})
        tx.save_response(scope, response)
# Commit succeeds before any response is sent.
return response</code></pre></div>
<div class="scene-foot">Helpers are illustrative. The transaction contains only local database work.</div>
</figure>
<div class="prose">
<p>A crash before commit rolls everything back. A crash after commit leaves both the order and its replayable response intact. A concurrent duplicate waits on the unique constraint, then reads the committed record. Bound that wait; if it expires, return a documented retryable outcome rather than starting the work again.</p>
<aside class="callout">An external payment call cannot join this local transaction. Commit an outbox task with the order, then deliver it using a stable downstream idempotency key. The receiver must enforce its own deduplication contract.</aside>
</div>
</section>
<section aria-labelledby="contract"><div class="prose"><div class="tag">03 / Boundaries</div><h2 id="contract">Specify when the promise ends</h2>
<p>Retention is part of correctness. Once a record expires, a late retry can become new work. Choose a retention window that covers client retries, queued redelivery, and operational recovery. Longer-lived business constraints may still be necessary: a unique merchant order reference can prevent duplication after the request ledger has been pruned.</p>
<p>Write the remaining behavior into the API contract:</p>
<ol class="step-list"><li><strong>Replay policy.</strong> Define which status codes and response fields are stored. Stripe, for example, <a href="https://docs.stripe.com/api/idempotent_requests">replays stored results including server errors</a>; your policy must be equally explicit.</li><li><strong>Retry budget.</strong> Use bounded backoff with jitter for transient failures. A parameter conflict requires correction, not another automatic attempt.</li><li><strong>Recovery evidence.</strong> Log the operation key, attempt identifier, and committed resource identifier separately. Operators need to distinguish repeated delivery from repeated effects.</li></ol>
<p>Test the lost-response boundary deliberately: commit, drop the connection, and resend the original key. Also race two identical requests and reuse a key with changed input. Those tests establish the promise that a happy-path retry cannot.</p>
</div></section>
<footer class="prose">
<h2>Keep the intention stable</h2>
<ul class="chain"><li>Name the work once.</li><li>Commit its effect and memory together.</li><li>Retry within an explicit contract.</li></ul>
<p class="design-note">Design note: cobalt carries emphasis and navigation; green marks committed effects, and amber marks uncertain delivery. Each semantic role has a paired tint in both themes, keeping delivery uncertainty distinct from durable state.</p>
